"Blues Jam @ Phil Brady's Bar & Grill","One of the longest continuously running blues jams in the country running weekly since 1986. Local and touring blues musicians sit in with the house band all night long.","9:00 PM","Phil Brady's Bar & Grill","4848 Government Street, Baton Rouge, LA 70806","Thursdays","","House band; bring your own instrument","In Person from 9:00 PM","https://www.facebook.com/philbradys.bar/","<h2>Phil Brady's Bar & Grill</h2><p>Phil Brady's Bar & Grill is a mid-city Baton Rouge institution housed in a building dating to the 1940s, when it operated as Michael's Bar. The current nightclub has been open since 1978 under longtime owner Joe Hall, and it still carries the original bar, railing and mirrors from its earlier life. It is best known across Louisiana as the home of one of the longest continuously running blues jams in the country.</p><h3>Atmosphere and Setting</h3><p>Phil Brady's is a no-frills neighborhood bar with a stage tucked into one corner, pool tables, and a crowd that ranges from longtime regulars to touring musicians passing through Baton Rouge. The room has an unpretentious, lived-in feel that has made it a comfortable home base for the city's blues community for decades.</p><h3>The Thursday Night Blues Jam</h3><p>Founded in 1986 by Shannon &ldquo;Bayou&rdquo; Williford, the weekly Thursday blues jam has run virtually every week since, celebrating its 40th anniversary in 2025. The jam has served as a training ground for generations of Baton Rouge blues musicians, some of whom went on to national careers, and it continues to draw surprise sit-ins from touring artists passing through town. As one regular put it, at Brady's &ldquo;you never know who you're gonna see.&rdquo;</p><h3>Other Programming</h3><p>Beyond the Thursday jam, Phil Brady's regularly books local blues, rock and roots acts throughout the week and is a frequent stop on the Baton Rouge Blues Festival's satellite events calendar.</p><h3>For Performers</h3><p>The jam is a true walk-up, sit-in format built around a rotating house band, welcoming musicians of every experience level who want to play the blues alongside seasoned players.</p><h3>Good to Know</h3><p>Phil Brady's Bar & Grill is located at 4848 Government Street, Baton Rouge, LA 70806. Street parking is available around the building.</p>"
"Songwriter Open Mic @ Le Chien Brewing Company","A twice-monthly acoustic open mic hosted by local musician Victoria Lea inside a dog-friendly Denham Springs craft brewery welcoming original songs and covers.","6:00 PM","Le Chien Brewing Company","101 S. Hummel St., Denham Springs, LA 70726","Wednesdays","Victoria Lea","","In Person from 6:00 PM","http://www.lechien.beer/","<h2>Le Chien Brewing Company</h2><p>Le Chien Brewing Company is a dog-friendly craft brewery in downtown Denham Springs, a Livingston Parish city just east of Baton Rouge that anchors the eastern edge of the metro area's live music and craft beer scene. The taproom pours a rotating lineup of house-brewed beers alongside a food menu of pub favorites including wings, and it has become a regular gathering spot for local musicians and songwriters.</p><h3>Atmosphere and Setting</h3><p>The brewery has a relaxed, family- and pet-friendly taproom vibe, with indoor and patio seating in the heart of Denham Springs' small but growing downtown entertainment district.</p><h3>Songwriter Open Mic</h3><p>On the first and third Wednesday of each month, Le Chien hosts a Songwriter Open Mic led by local host Victoria Lea, inviting acoustic performers and songwriters from around the Baton Rouge area to share original material and covers in a welcoming, low-key setting over craft beer.</p><h3>Other Programming</h3><p>Le Chien also partners with local businesses on community events such as book-and-brew nights, and regularly features other live acoustic performers on non-open-mic nights.</p><h3>For Performers</h3><p>The open mic leans toward solo and acoustic singer-songwriter performances, making it a good fit for original material as well as covers.</p><h3>Good to Know</h3><p>Le Chien Brewing Company is located at 101 S. Hummel St., Denham Springs, LA 70726, about 20 minutes east of downtown Baton Rouge.</p>"
"Old Time Music Open Jam @ West Baton Rouge Museum","A monthly acoustic jam led by Kent Louque featuring guitars fiddles mandolins and string bass playing early to mid-20th-century country tunes. All skill levels welcome to sit in.","2:00 PM","West Baton Rouge Museum","845 North Jefferson Avenue, Port Allen, LA 70767","Sundays","Kent Louque","","In Person","https://westbatonrougemuseum.org/870/Old-Time-Music-Open-Jam","<h2>West Baton Rouge Museum</h2><p>The West Baton Rouge Museum, nicknamed the &ldquo;Sugarcane Museum,&rdquo; sits on a historic parish complex in Port Allen just across the Mississippi River from downtown Baton Rouge. Alongside exhibits on the region's sugar industry and a collection of preserved plantation-era buildings, the museum has become a well-loved home for grassroots acoustic music traditions in the greater Baton Rouge area.</p><h3>Atmosphere and Setting</h3><p>Jams take place in an air-conditioned museum meeting room, with sessions occasionally moving to a historic Juke Joint on the museum grounds during cooler months. The setting is casual, family-friendly and rooted in the region's cultural history.</p><h3>Old Time Music Open Jam</h3><p>On the second Sunday of every month, guitarist Kent Louque leads an Old Time Music Open Jam built around early to mid-20th-century country songs. Attendance has ranged from a handful of pickers to more than thirty musicians strumming guitars, fiddles, mandolins and string bass, with new players of any skill level encouraged to join in.</p><h3>Cajun Music Jam</h3><p>The following week, on the third Sunday of the month, the Cajun French Music Association hosts a companion Cajun Jam at the museum, keeping South Louisiana's Cajun French musical tradition alive with fiddle, accordion and guitar players of all levels welcome to sit in.</p><h3>Other Programming</h3><p>The museum's broader calendar includes historical exhibits, a working 1904 World's Fair sugar mill model display, blacksmith demonstrations, and seasonal community events throughout the year.</p><h3>For Performers</h3><p>Both monthly jams are informal, drop-in sessions with no sign-up required &mdash; musicians are simply invited to bring an instrument and join the circle.</p><h3>Good to Know</h3><p>The West Baton Rouge Museum is located at 845 North Jefferson Avenue, Port Allen, LA 70767, just a few minutes from downtown Baton Rouge across the Horace Wilkinson Bridge.</p>"
